{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: design the dramatic simplification of the web UI, as a ratifiable note answering one question hugh posed on 2026-08-19: what is the SIMPLEST and most compact effective interface for a human operator to see what is unclaimed, unfinished, and otherwise active? The current Work view fails this: it announces counts like \"57 active\" (every actor's open, promised and reported work) with flat filters and no way to prioritize or discern importance, and the thread view is massively complex for routine tasks. Hugh's direction, which the note should refine rather than relitigate: the primary list is oriented around top-level threads — open requests — one short single-line entry each; clicking an entry opens the thread view; the thread view itself must also be radically simplified. Current weight for calibration: Stream.tsx 1,020 lines, ThreadPane.tsx 674, WorkDrawer.tsx 635, plus two railway visualizations. Design only — no source edits until the note is ratified. (Corrected refile of ac9c7796, which carried an unresolvable rests_on reference.)","body":{"conditions":"1) Deliverable is one short plain-English design note under notes/ proposing the simplest compact UI for operator awareness. It must contain: a screen inventory saying explicitly what is deleted and what survives, with every surviving element justified against the operator question 'what waits, and on whom?'; a one-line row specification for the top-level list keyed on open requests (state such as unclaimed / in progress / reported / needs-attention, who it waits on, age); the ordering rule that puts unclaimed and waiting-on-a-human work first; and a thread-view specification reduced to the commitment spine (request, promise, artifact/report, review verdict, merge or ratification) with provenance hops, superseded repair chains, and chat collapsed behind explicit expansion, such that an operator can tell what a thread waits on within one screen. 2) Simplicity is the acceptance criterion: prefer deletion over configuration, and no count may appear anywhere without being a click away from exactly the rows it counts. 3) The design composes with the staleness-presentation principle of request 427b3bd3 (ordinary staleness quiet, world-staleness loud) and may assume the enriched row fields of request c4d7ce8c. 4) No runtime or source changes until ratified: report the note ready for ratification by hugh; implementation follows as a separate request resting on the ratified decision. Produce the note on a request/\u003cslug\u003e branch with an exact-head artifact per the discipline.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}